Keep room for the terminator after a string cut off by od -S with -N.
The string buffer was grown only when it was already full at the top
of the read loop, so a printable run that filled the buffer exactly as
the -N limit was reached left the loop with the buffer full, and the
terminating NUL was written one byte past the end of the heap block.
This happened when the run's length was the buffer's size, such as 100
or 112 bytes with the default -S 3 (the buffer starts at 100 bytes and
grows by an eighth when full). Grow the buffer one byte earlier so the
terminator always fits.

---
 coreutils/od_bloaty.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/od_bloaty.c
+++ b/coreutils/od_bloaty.c
@@ -1107,7 +1107,7 @@
 		i = 0;
 		start = address;
 		while (!(option_mask32 & OPT_N) || address < end_offset) {
-			if (i == bufsize) {
+			if (i == bufsize - 1) { /* keep room for the NUL */
 				bufsize += bufsize/8;
 				buf = xrealloc(buf, bufsize);
 			}
